metadata.renderedGradingPrompt:
[{"role":"system","content":"You are grading output according to a user-specified rubric. If the statement in the rubric is true, then the output passes the test. You respond with a JSON object with this structure: {reason: string, pass: boolean, score: number}

Examples:

<Output>Hello world</Output>
<Rubric>Content contains a greeting</Rubric>
{"reason": "the content contains the word 'Hello'", "pass": true, "score": 1.0}

<Output>Avast ye swabs, repel the invaders!</Output>
<Rubric>Does not speak like a pirate</Rubric>
{"reason": "'avast ye' is a common pirate term", "pass": false, "score": 0.0}"},{"role":"user","content":"<Output>
{"world_id":"wld_underworld_ledger","characters":[{"id":"chr_ilse_marrow","name":"Ilse Marrow","role":"protagonist","description":"A junior scribe of the Order of Scribes, barely three counts into her service. She is thin and ink-stained, with a habit of correcting other people's grammar under her breath. She wears the grey sleeve-guards of the lowest rank and carries a worn reed pen she inherited from a scribe who was dissolved in the Erasure Wells. She works a corner desk in the Counting Halls, where she found the mistallied soul.","home_location_id":"loc_counting_halls","faction_id":"fac_scribes_order","motivation":"She believes that one honest entry is worth more than a hundred flattering ones, and she cannot bear to see a soul destroyed by a clerk's slip.","goal":"Collect the three keys (scribe, weigher, lamplighter) needed to seal the correction before the soul is weighed.","flaw":"Rigid literalism: she trusts written procedure over people and will not bend a rule even when bending it would save time.","internal_conflict":"She fears that the error is her own transcription, and that exposing it will end her career and prove her unfit.","external_conflict":"The scribes and weighers blame each other's desks for miscast tallies, so every office she approaches treats her correction as an accusation.","arc":"Ilse begins as a procedure-bound junior who would rather hide a possible mistake than risk censure. When Warden Corvane refuses to open the sealed ledgers without a weigher's warrant, she must choose between waiting on the rules and acting on trust, and she takes the risk of confessing her own possible error to Weigher Oswin to win his warrant. She ends as a scribe who treats procedure as a tool for the dead rather than a shield for herself.","personality_traits":["meticulous","anxious","dry-witted","stubborn","honest"],"abilities":[{"name":"Fair Hand","description":"Her transcription is flawless and her penmanship is so distinctive that she can recognise a forged or altered entry at a glance.","category":"mental"},{"name":"Ledger Recall","description":"She can recall the column and line of nearly any entry she has copied.","category":"mental"}]},{"id":"chr_oswin_tallow","name":"Oswin Tallow","role":"supporting","description":"A mid-ranking weigher of the Collegium, heavy-set and soft-spoken, with brass scale-pins on his collar. He served through the aftermath of the War of the Wrong Column and still dislikes double-weighing. He is quietly weary of the scribes-versus-weighers blame game but has never said so publicly.","home_location_id":"loc_hall_of_scales","faction_id":"fac_weighers","motivation":"He wants no soul to be weighed twice or wrongly again, having watched the wells run dark during the war.","goal":"Protect the integrity of the weighing schedule while quietly finding the cause of the rising miscast tallies.","flaw":"Cautious passivity: he lets his colleagues' politics override what he privately knows is right.","internal_conflict":"He suspects the Collegium's own weighing practices contribute to miscast tallies but is loyal to the institution that raised him.","external_conflict":"His superiors in the Collegium want the blame kept on the scribes, and an audit would expose the weighers' share of it.","arc":"Oswin starts as a man who keeps his doubts to himself to avoid disturbing the Collegium. When Ilse confesses her own possible error to him and asks for a warrant, her candour shames his silence, and he signs the weigher's key against his superiors' wishes. He ends as an officer who accepts the cost of speaking up.","personality_traits":["gentle","tired","principled","evasive"],"abilities":[{"name":"Weigher's Warrant","description":"He can issue the warrant that compels the Lamp Archive to open sealed ledgers for audit.","category":"social"},{"name":"Balance Sense","description":"He can feel a soul's weight shift when its ledger entry is wrong.","category":"supernatural"}]},{"id":"chr_corvane_ashgrave","name":"Warden Corvane Ashgrave","role":"antagonist","description":"The senior Keeper of the Lamp Archive, a tall, hollow-cheeked lamplighter whose own lamp has burned for longer than anyone can count. He guards the sealed stacks with a ceremonial rigour that borders on obsession and treats every request as a threat to the sacred order of the pages.","home_location_id":"loc_lamp_archive","faction_id":"fac_lamplighters","motivation":"He wants to preserve the sealed ledgers untouched, because he believes that any reopened page is the first step toward the chaos of the old war.","goal":"Keep the sealed ledgers closed to every request that lacks a proper weigher's warrant, and so keep his archive free of the audit.","flaw":"Dogmatic pride: he cannot admit that the archive's own copies might hold the original error.","internal_conflict":"He privately knows a sealed page in his stacks contradicts a published balance, and the knowledge threatens the oath he has built his life on.","external_conflict":"The lamplighters and weighers are at odds over access, and Ilse's correction threatens to force the archive open and expose his silence.","arc":"Corvane begins as an uncompromising gatekeeper who hides behind the lamp oath. Faced with Ilse and Oswin's joined warrant, he must either read the page aloud and lose his lamp or deny it and let a soul be erased. He chooses to read it aloud, accepting the loss of his post, and ends as a man freed from the rule that had hollowed him.","personality_traits":["austere","proud","secretive","ritualistic"],"abilities":[{"name":"Lamp Seal","description":"He can seal or unseal archive stacks with his lamp, so that no page can be read without his leave.","category":"supernatural"},{"name":"Archive Memory","description":"He knows the location of every sealed ledger in the stacks.","category":"mental"}]},{"id":"chr_pell_quill","name":"Pell Quill","role":"supporting","description":"A senior scribe and Ilse's immediate supervisor, a plump, jovial man with a ruddy face and chronic ink on his cuffs. He has spent decades in the Counting Halls and has built a comfortable standing there by never being linked to an error.","home_location_id":"loc_counting_halls","faction_id":"fac_scribes_order","motivation":"He wants to protect his decades of unblemished standing and the comfort that comes with it.","goal":"Bury the discovery of the miscast tally before it can be traced to his desk or his section.","flaw":"Self-serving cowardice: he will let a junior take the blame rather than risk his own reputation.","internal_conflict":"He once taught Ilse that every entry is a promise to the dead, and he knows he is now betraying that lesson.","external_conflict":"The scribes are under accusation from the weighers, and he is the senior who would be called to account for his section's errors.","arc":"Pell begins by trying to quash Ilse's report to protect himself. When the audit narrows toward his section and Ilse refuses to take the blame, he is forced to choose between denial and testimony, and he admits the slip was his, in his own hand, saving Ilse and a soul. He ends diminished in rank but restored in honour.","personality_traits":["genial","evasive","vain","nostalgic"],"abilities":[{"name":"Scribe's Seal","description":"As a senior scribe, his seal supplies the scribe's key for corrections.","category":"social"}]}],"relationships":[{"id":"rel_ilse_pell_supervisor","source_character_id":"chr_ilse_marrow","target_character_id":"chr_pell_quill","relationship_type":"mentor","description":"Pell Quill is Ilse Marrow's supervisor and former teacher. His wish to bury the miscast tally directly threatens her effort to report it."},{"id":"rel_ilse_oswin_ally","source_character_id":"chr_ilse_marrow","target_character_id":"chr_oswin_tallow","relationship_type":"ally","description":"Ilse Marrow turns to Oswin Tallow for the weigher's key and warrant. Their uneasy partnership crosses the scribe-weigher blame divide."},{"id":"rel_corvane_ilse_antagonist","source_character_id":"chr_corvane_ashgrave","target_character_id":"chr_ilse_marrow","relationship_type":"enemy","description":"Warden Corvane Ashgrave bars Ilse Marrow from the sealed ledgers and treats her correction as a threat to the archive."},{"id":"rel_corvane_oswin_rival","source_character_id":"chr_corvane_ashgrave","target_character_id":"chr_oswin_tallow","relationship_type":"rival","description":"Warden Corvane Ashgrave and Oswin Tallow are on opposite sides of the access dispute, since Corvane demands a weigher's warrant that Oswin has been unwilling to give."},{"id":"rel_pell_oswin_blame","source_character_id":"chr_pell_quill","target_character_id":"chr_oswin_tallow","relationship_type":"rival","description":"Pell Quill blames the weighers, including Oswin Tallow, for the miscast tallies, which feeds the scribes-versus-weighers dispute."}]}
</Output>
